  {
    "index": 3,
    "hash": "9eXWGZSkUsIquYDE9C+w9NV+Qkw=",
    "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-14T18:11:36.000Z",
    "action": {
      "type": "SET_CONTENT",
      "input": {
        "content": "DWG files are building/construction CAD files **without semantic meaning** \u2014 these days typically generated from RVT or IFC files as 2D drawings, cuts, and projections. IFC is the BIM standard for 3D construction files **with semantic meaning**; Autodesk Revit's RVT is the most common proprietary 3D format.\n\nThe industry is mid-transition: from 2D-DWG-first workflows to building 3D BIM models first and deriving 2D plans from them, so that elements \u2014 a door, a duct \u2014 carry semantic tags rather than being anonymous line work (DT BAU walkthrough, 00:11:26\u201300:14:11).\n\nTwo practical consequences:\n- DWG survives as an interchange and precise-measurement format, not as the source of truth.\n- The construction toolchain is 90\u201395% Windows because Revit and most industry tools lack Mac support; Mac-based architecture studios fall back to DWG interchange to participate (00:35:39).\n\nSemantic 3D-first modeling is what makes model data machine-readable at all \u2014 every automation ambition downstream (collision checks, AI review, structured BCF workflows) depends on it.",
        "updatedAt": "2026-08-14T18:11:36.886961+00:00"
      },
      "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-14T18:11:36.000Z"
    }
  },
